  "prediction": "**Overseas Contingency Operations (OCO) Funding: A Review of the Past Two Decades**\n\nSince the 9/11 terrorist attacks, the US government has allocated approximately $2 trillion in discretionary budget authority for Overseas Contingency Operations (OCO) and emergency funding. This report provides an overview of the OCO funding process, its evolution, and the current state of affairs.\n\n**OCO Funding Breakdown**\n\nBetween FY2001 and FY2019, Congress has appropriated $2 trillion in OCO funding, with the majority ($1.8 trillion) going to the Department of Defense (DOD). The State Department and USAID have received $154 billion, while the Department of Homeland Security and the Coast Guard have received $3 billion.\n\n**OCO Funding Trends**\n\nThe OCO funding has increased significantly since 2001, peaking at $68 billion in FY2008. However, the funding has decreased in recent years, with FY2019 OCO funding totaling $68.8 billion. The DOD has received the majority of OCO funding, with the Army, Navy, and Air Force being the largest recipients.\n\n**OCO Funding Designation**\n\nThe OCO funding designation was created to provide flexibility for the executive branch to respond to emerging crises. However, critics argue that the designation has been used to circumvent budget caps and avoid transparency. The OCO funding is not subject to the same budget constraints as regular appropriations, allowing the executive branch to allocate funds without congressional oversight.\n\n**Future of OCO Funding**\n\nThe future of OCO funding is uncertain, with the 116th Congress considering legislation to reform the OCO funding process. The National Defense Authorization Act for FY2020 includes provisions to reduce OCO funding and increase transparency. The Congressional Budget Office (CBO) estimates that OCO funding will decrease to $50 billion by FY2025.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe OCO funding process has evolved significantly since 2001, with the majority of funding going to the DOD. However, the lack of transparency and accountability has raised concerns among lawmakers and budget experts. As the US continues to face emerging crises, the OCO funding process must be reformed to ensure that it is used effectively and efficiently.",
